\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every integer $N\geq 2$, does the gauge-invariant relative SU(N) BFSS Hamiltonian, with its source-defined self-adjoint domain and fermion grading, have a one-dimensional zero-energy kernel consisting of Spin(9)-invariant even states?

\subsection{Short English statement}
For every integer $N\geq 2$, does the gauge-invariant relative SU(N) BFSS Hamiltonian, with its source-defined self-adjoint domain and fermion grading, have a one-dimensional zero-energy kernel consisting of Spin(9)-invariant even states?

